    <h2 class="section-title">4.1 LLM Fundamentals Every Modern Developer Must Know</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">Q 4.1.1</span> Core Concepts: Tokens, Context Windows, and Temperature</div>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Tokens:</b> Basic atomic units of text processed by an LLM (~1 token ≈ 0.75 English words or 4 characters). Billing and model limits are strictly calculated in tokens.</li>
        <li><b>Context Window:</b> The maximum number of combined input (prompt) and output (completion) tokens the model can process in a single request (e.g. 128k tokens in GPT-4o).</li>
        <li><b>Temperature:</b> Controls randomness/creativity of generations:
          <ul>
            <li><code>Temperature = 0.0 - 0.2</code>: Highly deterministic, precise, and factual. <b>Mandatory for code generation, JSON parsing, and financial data</b>.</li>
            <li><code>Temperature = 0.7 - 1.0</code>: Creative, diverse text generation.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-title">4.2 Connecting C# .NET to AI Services: Semantic Kernel</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">Q 4.2.1</span> Microsoft Semantic Kernel vs. Direct OpenAI SDK</div>
      <p><b>Semantic Kernel (SK):</b> Microsoft's official open-source enterprise SDK that integrates LLMs (OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Hugging Face) with conventional C# code. It provides built-in support for <b>Plugins (Native C# Functions), Memory (Vector Stores), and Planners/Agents</b>.</p>

      <div class="code-box">// Integrating Semantic Kernel in ASP.NET Core
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.ChatCompletion;

var builder = Kernel.CreateBuilder();
builder.AddAzureOpenAIChatCompletion(
    deploymentName: "gpt-4o",
    endpoint: "https://my-openai-resource.openai.azure.com/",
    apiKey: "my-azure-api-key"
);

Kernel kernel = builder.Build();
var chatService = kernel.GetRequiredService&lt;IChatCompletionService&gt;();

var chatHistory = new ChatHistory();
chatHistory.AddSystemMessage("You are an expert enterprise .NET software architect.");
chatHistory.AddUserMessage("Explain why ValueTask reduces allocations in hot paths.");

// Streaming response token-by-token in C# using IAsyncEnumerable
await foreach (var messagePart in chatService.GetStreamingChatMessageContentsAsync(chatHistory))
{
    Console.Write(messagePart.Content);
}</div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-title">4.3 Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Architecture</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">Q 4.3.1</span> What is RAG and Why is it Preferred Over Model Fine-Tuning?</div>
      <p><b>Problem:</b> LLMs have knowledge cut-off dates and frequently <b>hallucinate</b> when asked about proprietary private enterprise databases, HR policies, or live inventory.</p>
      
      <p><b>Fine-Tuning vs. RAG:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Fine-Tuning:</b> Retrains model weights. Extremely expensive, slow, prone to catastrophic forgetting, and cannot guarantee real-time access to changing database records.</li>
        <li><b>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation):</b> Dynamically searches and retrieves relevant factual excerpts from private company data and injects them directly into the prompt context window before calling the LLM. <b>Factual, up-to-date, cost-effective, and fully auditable with citations!</b></li>
      </ul>

      <p><b>The 4-Step RAG Pipeline:</b></p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Data Ingestion & Chunking:</b> Split large PDFs or database tables into semantic chunks (e.g., 500 tokens with 50-token overlap).</li>
        <li><b>Embedding Generation:</b> Pass chunks into an embedding model (e.g. <code>text-embedding-3-small</code>) to generate 1536-dimensional mathematical vector representations.</li>
        <li><b>Vector Storage:</b> Store vectors and chunk text into a Vector Database (Qdrant, pgvector, Azure AI Search, Pinecone).</li>
        <li><b>Retrieval & Augmented Prompting:</b> When user asks a question, convert their question to a vector, find the top 3 nearest chunks via <b>Cosine Similarity</b>, and pass them as context to the LLM:</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="code-box">Prompt to LLM:
"Answer the user question using ONLY the provided context below. If not found, say 'I don't know'.
CONTEXT: {Retrieved Vector Search Chunks}
USER QUESTION: {User Question}"</div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-title">4.4 Function Calling / Tool Calling in .NET</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">Q 4.4.1</span> How LLMs Trigger Native C# Functions</div>
      <p><b>Function Calling:</b> Allows an LLM to recognize that a user request requires external data or action (e.g., "Check order status for ID 1098"). The LLM does <i>not</i> execute the code itself; instead, it outputs structured JSON specifying the function name and extracted arguments. The .NET backend executes the real C# method and feeds the result back to the model.</p>

      <div class="code-box">// Exposing a C# Method as an AI Tool using Semantic Kernel
public class OrderPlugins
{
    [KernelFunction, Description("Fetches live delivery status of an order from SQL database")]
    public async Task&lt;string&gt; GetOrderStatus(
        [Description("The unique integer Order ID")] int orderId)
    {
        // Real C# database query executed securely on backend:
        return $"Order #{orderId} is currently Out for Delivery via BlueDart.";
    }
}</div>
    </div>
    
  
