    <h2 class="section-title">5.1 The 90-Second Winning Self-Introduction (Elevator Pitch)</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">HR 1</span> "Tell Me About Yourself" (Tailored for a 3 YOE .NET Full-Stack Developer)</div>
      
      <div class="callout-interview">
        <div class="callout-interview-title">🎯 Structure of a Master-Class 90-Second Intro:</div>
        <b>Present (Who you are today & Core Tech)</b> ➔ <b>Past (Foundation & Key Projects)</b> ➔ <b>Key Impact (Quantified Business Value)</b> ➔ <b>Future (Why you are here today)</b>.
      </div>

      <div class="code-box" style="background-color: #F8FAFC; border-left: 4px solid #1E3A8A; color: #1E293B;">
"Good morning / afternoon! Thank you for this opportunity.

My name is <b>Mahesh Gupta</b>. I am a <b>Full-Stack .NET Developer with over 3 years of hands-on industry experience</b> specializing in building secure, scalable web applications and RESTful microservices using <b>C#, ASP.NET Core, SQL Server, and Angular</b>.

In my current role, I actively contribute across the entire Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC). On the backend, I design and develop high-throughput REST APIs, implement Clean Architecture and Repository patterns, and optimize complex database operations using Entity Framework Core and raw SQL Server queries. On the frontend, I build modular, responsive user interfaces using Angular with Reactive Forms, RxJS streams, and HTTP interceptors.

One of the proudest highlights of my work was leading the optimization of our core reporting API. By analyzing SQL Server execution plans, creating targeted covering indexes, and converting synchronous blocking database calls into asynchronous async/await workflows with Redis caching, we <b>reduced API response latency by nearly 55%</b> and eliminated recurring database connection timeouts during peak business hours.

I also continuously upskill in modern technologies, including integrating AI and LLM capabilities into backend workflows and containerizing microservices.

Now, with 3 years of solid foundation, I am looking to transition to an organization like yours where I can take on greater technical ownership, contribute to high-impact distributed systems, and continue growing as a senior full-stack engineer. That's a brief summary of my journey!"</div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-title">5.2 "Why Do You Want to Change Your Company?"</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">HR 2</span> Why are You Looking for a Change? (The Diplomatic, High-Value Answer)</div>
      
      <div class="callout-warning">
        <div class="callout-warning-title">⚠️ What NOT to Say:</div>
        Never complain about low salary, poor management, heavy workload, or internal company politics. The interviewer evaluates your professionalism and attitude under pressure.
      </div>

      <div class="code-box" style="background-color: #F0FDF4; border-left: 4px solid #16A34A; color: #1E293B;">
<b>The Winning Response:</b>

"My journey with my current organization has been truly rewarding. Over the past 3 years, they gave me the platform to grow from a junior developer into a dependable full-stack engineer, and I've built strong relationships with my team and managers.

However, I feel I have reached a learning plateau in terms of architectural scope on my current project. At this 3-year juncture in my career, I am eager to step out of my comfort zone and challenge myself in a more dynamic technical environment.

I want to work on large-scale distributed architectures, deeper cloud-native solutions, and high-concurrency systems. When I reviewed this role and your engineering culture, I saw a direct alignment with my technical aspirations where I can make immediate meaningful contributions while continuing to elevate my craft."</div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-title">5.3 "What Are Your Strengths and Weaknesses?"</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">HR 3</span> Strengths and Strategic Weakness with Turnaround Story</div>

      <p><b>Strengths (Always back with real evidence):</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Root-Cause Problem Solving:</b> "I don't just patch symptoms; I dig into SQL execution plans and memory profilers to resolve the fundamental bottleneck."</li>
        <li><b>End-to-End Ownership:</b> "I comfortably bridge backend API logic with frontend Angular components, ensuring smooth API contracts and robust error handling."</li>
      </ul>

      <p><b>Strategic Weakness (Genuine, non-critical, with active improvement):</b></p>
      <div class="code-box" style="background-color: #FFFBEB; border-left: 4px solid #D97706; color: #1E293B;">
<b>The Honest & Professional Weakness Response:</b>

"Earlier in my career, I had a tendency to spend too much time over-engineering solutions—trying to make every single utility class ultra-generic from day one, which sometimes extended delivery timelines.

I recognized this and adopted a more pragmatic approach: <i>'Make it work, make it clean, make it fast'</i>. Now, I focus on delivering clean, working code that meets immediate business requirements first, and refactor for broader generality only when actual domain need arises. This has significantly accelerated my delivery velocity."</div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-title">5.4 Real-Life STAR Method Behavioral Scenarios</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">HR 4</span> "Describe a Critical Production Bug You Resolved Under Pressure" (STAR Story)</div>
      
      <ul>
        <li><b>S - Situation:</b> "During a month-end financial closing, our order processing API started failing with HTTP 500 errors. Customers experienced frequent timeouts, and server CPU spiked to 95%."</li>
        <li><b>T - Task:</b> "As the on-call .NET engineer, I was tasked with identifying the root cause, stopping the transaction failures immediately, and ensuring no customer orders were lost."</li>
        <li><b>A - Action:</b>
          <ol>
            <li>I checked our application logs and SQL Server Profiler, where I spotted multiple <b>SQL Error 1205: Transaction was deadlocked on resources with another process</b>.</li>
            <li>I analyzed the queries involved and discovered that two concurrent background jobs were updating the <code>Orders</code> and <code>Inventory</code> tables in reverse order (Job 1 locked Orders then Inventory; Job 2 locked Inventory then Orders).</li>
            <li>Furthermore, the API was using synchronous <code>.Result</code> on an async database call, leading to thread pool starvation.</li>
            <li>I hotfixed the access order to be strictly sequential across both jobs, enabled <code>READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT</code> (RCSI) on the database, and refactored the blocking API call to proper <code>await</code> syntax with cancellation tokens.</li>
          </ol>
        </li>
        <li><b>R - Result:</b> "The hotfix deployed in 45 minutes, deadlocks dropped to zero, CPU utilization normalized back to 28%, and all pending transactions processed successfully. I documented the post-mortem incident report and added automated concurrency tests."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">HR 5</span> "How Do You Handle Disagreements with QA or Product Managers?"</div>
      <div class="code-box" style="background-color: #F8FAFC; border-left: 4px solid #0284C7; color: #1E293B;">
"I view QA and Product Managers as partners working toward the same objective: customer satisfaction and product reliability. 

When a QA engineer flags an edge case that I initially believe is out of scope or not a defect, I never argue over chat or status tickets. I set up a quick 5-minute sync, walk through their exact reproduction steps, and understand the user impact. If it's a genuine risk, I happily fix it. If it's an enhancement outside the sprint commitment, I document it with the Product Owner for prioritized sprint backlog grooming. Clear communication and empathy always resolve tension."</div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-title">5.5 Career Vision, Notice Period & CTC Negotiation</h2>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">HR 6</span> Notice Period & Expected CTC Scripts</div>
      
      <p><b>Handling Notice Period (e.g. 60 or 90 days):</b></p>
      <div class="code-box">"My official notice period is [X] days as per company policy. However, I have already documented my project modules thoroughly. Once an official offer is finalized, I am confident I can initiate knowledge transfer (KT) immediately and request my management for an early release / buyout to join within [30/45 days]."</div>

      <p><b>Handling Expected CTC:</b></p>
      <div class="code-box">"My current compensation is [X] LPA. Based on my 3 years of full-stack experience in C#, .NET Core, SQL Server, and Angular, and considering the industry standard for this position and the responsibilities required, I am expecting around [Y] LPA (typically 30-40% hike or competitive market band). However, I am open to discussing a fair package that aligns with your company's compensation structure."</div>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
      <div class="question-title"><span class="q-badge">HR 7</span> Smart Questions YOU Must Ask the Interviewer at the End</div>
      <ol>
        <li><i>"What does the day-to-day architecture workflow look like for the engineering team on this project?"</i></li>
        <li><i>"What is the biggest technical challenge your team is currently tackling this quarter?"</i></li>
        <li><i>"How does your engineering team approach automated CI/CD pipelines, code reviews, and technical debt?"</i></li>
        <li><i>"What qualities distinguish a good developer from an exceptional developer in this role within the first 6 months?"</i></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    

  <!-- APPENDIX -->
  <div class="part-banner">
    <div style="font-size: 13pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 2px; opacity: 0.85;">Appendix</div>
    <h1 style="color: white; margin: 8px 0 4px 0; font-size: 24pt;">Interview Quick-Reference Cheat Sheets</h1>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section-title">A.1 C# Collections Time & Space Complexity</h2>
  <table class="custom-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Collection</th>
        <th>Lookup by Index</th>
        <th>Lookup by Key / Value</th>
        <th>Insert / Add</th>
        <th>Memory Overhead</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><b>Array (<code>T[]</code>)</b></td>
        <td>O(1)</td>
        <td>O(N)</td>
        <td>N/A (Fixed size)</td>
        <td>Lowest (contiguous memory)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><b><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code></b></td>
        <td>O(1)</td>
        <td>O(N)</td>
        <td>O(1) amortized</td>
        <td>Low (doubles capacity on overflow)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><b><code>Dictionary&lt;TKey, TVal&gt;</code></b></td>
        <td>N/A</td>
        <td>O(1) average (O(N) worst)</td>
        <td>O(1) average</td>
        <td>Moderate (buckets + entries array)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><b><code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code></b></td>
        <td>N/A</td>
        <td>O(1) average</td>
        <td>O(1) average</td>
        <td>Moderate (hash table with no values)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 class="section-title">A.2 Final Interview Success Checklist for Mahesh Gupta</h2>
  <div class="callout-reallife">
    <div class="callout-reallife-title">🚀 24 Hours Before Interview Checklist:</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Practice delivering the <b>90-Second Self Introduction (Section 5.1)</b> aloud 3 times until completely natural.</li>
      <li>Review the <b>Method Hiding vs Overriding (Section 1.1.3)</b> and <b>Captive Dependency (Section 1.7.2)</b> code snippets.</li>
      <li>Be ready to sketch the <b>Clustered Index B-Tree vs Non-Clustered with INCLUDE (Section 2.1)</b>.</li>
      <li>Remember the difference between <b>switchMap</b> (cancels) and <b>exhaustMap</b> (ignores) in Angular RxJS (Section 3.3.2).</li>
      <li>Have your <b>STAR story on the production deadlock resolution (Section 5.4)</b> fresh in your mind.</li>
      <li>Stay confident: With 3 years of hands-on full-stack experience, you bring immense practical value to any engineering team!</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

</div>
